['Below is a sample C program based on your description. This program takes a single command-line argument, converts it to an integer, and checks if it's within the bounds of a predefined array. If so, it prints the element at that index; otherwise, it prints "invalid input".

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Check if exactly one command-line argument is provided
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <index>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Convert the input string to an integer
    int index = atoi(argv[1]);

    // Define an array with some sample values
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]); // Calculate the number of elements in the array

    // Check if the index is within the valid range [0, size-1]
    if (index >= 0 && index < size) {
        printf("Element at index %d: %d\n", index, arr[index]);
    } else {
        printf("Invalid input\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
- The program checks if exactly one command-line argument is provided. If not, it prints usage instructions and exits.
- It uses `atoi()` to convert the command-line argument into an integer.
- An array `arr` is defined with some sample values. You can modify this as needed.
- The size of the array is calculated using `sizeof`.
- The program checks if the provided index is within the valid range of the array indices (from 0 to `size - 1`). If so, it prints the element at that index; otherwise, it prints "Invalid input".

To compile and run this program, you can use:

```sh
